.SS "int32_t schultz_events_set_callback (\fBschultz_events\fP * events, \fBschultz_event_callback_fn\fP callback, void * host_context)"

.PP
Installs the host's callback function\&. Call once at startup\&. Until it is installed, events are routed and state is updated but nothing is delivered\&.
.PP
\fBAcross a language boundary, use the queue instead\&.\fP This one takes a function pointer, so a bound language pays an upcall for every mouse movement\&. schultz_events_set_queue and schultz_events_drain deliver the same events as one downcall a frame\&. Nothing is lost by choosing the queue: it records everything a callback would have been told\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIevents\fP The routing state\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIcallback\fP The host's entry point, or NULL to stop delivery\&. 
.br
\fIhost_context\fP Passed back to callback unchanged\&. May be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT when events is NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_events_hit_test (\fBschultz_events\fP * events, \fBschultz_point\fP point, \fBschultz_handle\fP * out_node)"

.PP
Finds the deepest node under a point\&. Overlays are searched first, topmost down, then the content tree\&. Within a subtree the last child is tried first, because later children paint on top\&. Nodes that are not visible are skipped entirely, along with their subtrees\&.
.PP
A disabled node is still returned: it blocks what is behind it rather than letting clicks fall through, which is what a user expects from a greyed out control\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIevents\fP The routing state\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoint\fP The position in window coordinates\&. 
.br
\fIout_node\fP Receives the node, or SCHULTZ_HANDLE_NONE when the point hits nothing\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_events_mouse_move (\fBschultz_events\fP * events, \fBschultz_point\fP point, uint32_t modifiers)"

.PP
Routes a pointer motion\&. Updates the hovered node, emitting leave and enter as it changes, and sets or clears SCHULTZ_STATE_HOVERED, which marks the affected nodes dirty\&. While a button is held, motion goes to the captured node as a drag instead\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIevents\fP The routing state\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoint\fP The new position in window coordinates\&. 
.br
\fImodifiers\fP Bit set of SCHULTZ_MOD_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

.SS "int32_t schultz_events_mouse_button (\fBschultz_events\fP * events, \fBschultz_point\fP point, uint32_t button, int32_t down, uint32_t modifiers)"

.PP
Routes a mouse button press or release\&. A press captures the node under the pointer, so the matching release goes to it wherever the pointer has travelled\&. A release over the same node also produces a click\&. A press outside a capturing overlay dismisses that overlay and is consumed rather than reaching what is underneath\&.
.PP
A press also decides where focus is\&. On a focusable node it moves there; on anything else, empty space or a label or a plain container, focus goes away\&. That is what closes an on-screen keyboard, which follows focus and is managed nowhere else, and it is what a desktop does too: click the empty canvas and the caret leaves the field\&. The secondary button is the exception, because it asks for a context menu rather than pressing what is under it\&.
.PP
A finger has to have stayed roughly still as well, or the release is not a click\&. Content that scrolls with a finger carries the row it started on along underneath it, so that row is still what the release lands on, and without this every scroll would also press whatever it began on\&. A mouse is unaffected: pressing a large button, moving the cursor inside it and letting go is a click, and a cursor does not scroll by dragging\&.
.PP
\fBParameters\fP
.RS 4
\fIevents\fP The routing state\&. Must not be NULL\&. 
.br
\fIpoint\fP The position in window coordinates\&. 
.br
\fIbutton\fP One of the SCHULTZ_BUTTON_* values\&. 
.br
\fIdown\fP Nonzero for a press, zero for a release\&. 
.br
\fImodifiers\fP Bit set of SCHULTZ_MOD_* values\&. 
.RE
.PP
\fBReturns\fP
.RS 4
SCHULTZ_OK, or SCHULTZ_ERR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\&. 
.RE
.PP

